info  {"category":"agent-session","raw":{"timestamp":"2026-08-07T13:13:17.918Z","type":"event_msg","
      payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"{\"summary\":\"Audit complete; no files were
      modified. Risk matrix and UX guardrails:\\n\\nCRITICAL — restore: Replaces managed runtime,
      configuration, installed MSW tools, and SSH material; stops workspaces but ignores stop
      failures; accepts a missing checksum with only a warning; preserves Keychain tokens even
      though restored GitHub metadata may authorize them; retains previous files for rollback and
      leaves VMs stopped (bin/msw:1470-1738). Relative escaping symlinks are not rejected, only
      absolute symlinks. Hide under Advanced. Require the existing typed RESTORE phrase; require a
      stronger RESTORE UNVERIFIED phrase when the checksum is absent. Describe a checksum as
      integrity evidence, not source authenticity. Accept only user-selected trusted archives, prove
      every VM stopped before mutation, block concurrent actions, and require GitHub re-verification
      afterward.\\n\\nCRITICAL — exec: Runs arbitrary argv in /workspace, auto-starts the VM, and
      can mutate or delete everything visible inside that workspace, including its Docker runtime
      (bin/msw:356-406,582-587). Hide under Advanced developer controls. Never build a shell string;
      pass argv exactly. Show workspace, argv, and auto-start impact, then require EXEC <workspace>.
      Never retry automatically after timeout or disconnect.\\n\\nHIGH — GitHub setup/verify/remove:
      These are mutating operations, not passive credential checks. Setup takes hidden tokens,
      stores them in Keychain, quarantines and stops the workspace before mutation, writes access
      metadata, restarts to bind the read secret, then attempts a guest push and—when host-write is
      enabled—creates, verifies, and deletes a real remote test branch. Failure attempts rollback;
      incomplete rollback keeps the workspace quarantined. Verify repeats the secret rebind/restart
      and remote branch exercise. Remove revokes host-write metadata first, restarts to remove the
      guest secret, then deletes both Keychain items; cleanup failure quarantines the workspace
      (bin/msw:1114-1363). Require explicit workspace/repository review; typed ENABLE HOST WRITE
      <workspace> for host-write setup, confirmation for read-only setup and verify, and REMOVE
      GITHUB <workspace> for removal. Warn that verification can leave an msw-permission-test-*
      branch if remote cleanup fails. Capture tokens only through a secure PTY/hidden field, never
      argv, environment, logs, analytics, clipboard history, or crash reports.\\n\\nHIGH —
      quarantine/lock trust boundary: setup, verify, and remove use a nonblocking per-workspace
      kernel lock; the lock file can remain after completion and must not itself be interpreted as
      busy. The inherited verifier retains the lock if its parent dies. Quarantine markers are
      written before risky credential changes; normal start/restart/exec/push paths fail closed and
      quarantine attempts to stop and prove the VM stopped (bin/msw:187-327,333-375). However
      bin/msw-ssh-proxy:27-59 never checks the quarantine marker, so direct SSH can serve an
      already-running quarantined VM or restart a stopped one when credentials permit. The app must
      disable all SSH-derived actions while quarantined, but the repository should be hardened
      before presenting quarantine as a complete security boundary. Show the stored quarantine
      reason prominently and offer only GitHub repair. Never clear the marker directly.\\n\\nHIGH —
      push: Requires host-write metadata and a Keychain write token, normalizes origin to
      github.com, fetches before prompting, pushes only the current committed branch through a
      verified bundle, excludes dirty files/tags/other branches, isolates host Git from ambient
      config/hooks/environment, and uses an exact observed-SHA force-with-lease (bin/msw:900-1102).
      Preserve the native PUSH confirmation for normal pushes; show owner/repo, path, branch, SHA,
      mode, and commit list from the same invocation. Force push belongs under Advanced and requires
      FORCE PUSH <owner/repo> <branch>. Do not expose --yes directly. Failures can be partial: LFS
      objects upload before the ref; the ref may succeed before the final guest fetch fails. On any
      post-initiation error, label outcome unknown, query the remote SHA, and never
      auto-retry.\\n\\nHIGH — clean: Defaults to all workspaces, auto-starts them, runs docker
      system prune -af plus builder prune, and --volumes permanently deletes every unused volume
      (bin/msw:843-865). Never default the UI to all. Require confirmation for ordinary prune, CLEAN
      ALL for all workspaces, and DELETE VOLUMES <workspace|ALL> for --volumes. Show that unused
      containers/images/networks/build cache are deleted even without --volumes. Report
      per-workspace partial completion.\\n\\nHIGH — host repair: Uses sudo and nontransactionally
      installs a root launch daemon/script, changes loopback aliases and /etc/hosts, flushes DNS,
      generates or reuses an unencrypted SSH private key, updates authorized_keys, and rewrites
      managed SSH configuration (bin/msw:655-781). Hide under Advanced; require REPAIR HOST, show
      every host-level target, expect a password prompt, and follow with quick/deep checks. A
      failure must be shown as partial host configuration, not safely rolled back.\\n\\nHIGH —
      upgrade/update: upgrade auto-starts targets and performs apt full-upgrade, autoremove, and
      clean; default target is all and earlier workspaces remain changed if a later one fails.
      update changes Homebrew metadata globally or runs msb self update, then doctor; Homebrew
      upgrade failure is explicitly ignored, so success may mean only doctor passed
      (bin/msw:605-623). Hide under Advanced maintenance. Require confirmation per workspace and
      UPGRADE ALL for all; show per-workspace progress. For update, record before/after versions and
      doctor separately and report partial/unchanged when the ignored upgrade failed.\\n\\nMEDIUM —
      backup: Creates a cold sparse archive containing runtime state, MSW configuration, GitHub
      metadata/quarantine state, and the SSH private key, but excludes Keychain tokens. It stops
      only workspaces that ping as running and tries to restart that set on every exit; restart
      failures are warnings and can coexist with “backup complete” (bin/msw:1470-1572). Require
      confirmation explaining interruption and archive sensitivity, destination/free-space review,
      and exclusive maintenance mode. Track archive creation, checksum creation, and each VM restart
      independently; “backup succeeded, restart incomplete” is a valid result.\\n\\nMEDIUM — resize:
      Passes memory/CPU values directly to msb modify without local format, capacity, or
      current-state validation (bin/msw:598-603); configured ceilings are in config.sh:22-36. Place
      in Advanced resources, show current/requested/ceiling and host headroom, validate locally, and
      require confirmation. Refresh actual values afterward; failure outcome is unknown until
      re-inspected.\\n\\nMEDIUM — tunnel: Validates ports, auto-starts the workspace, then holds an
      SSH local forward from localhost to guest 127.0.0.1; ExitOnForwardFailure confirms binding,
      not application health (bin/msw:625-633). Keep custom ports under Advanced, but no typed
      phrase is needed. Show local exposure, workspace/ports/PID/start time, distinguish “forward
      active” from “service healthy,” and provide an explicit stop action. Treat process
      disappearance as closed/stale.\\n\\nREAD-ONLY DEFAULT: Ambient status polling must use only
      non-starting observations: version, status/ps, fixed URL/port calculation, bounded logs, and
      direct inspect/status/ping equivalents. Do not poll repos, disk, check --deep, shell/Zed/open,
      tunnel, or any guest command because ensure_running can start a VM. GitHub status is logically
      read-only but calls Keychain for credential presence and reports only presence plus
      verification repo—not access mode, configured time, quarantine, secret binding, or last
      verification (bin/msw:1311-1320); run Keychain checks on demand or cache briefly to avoid
      background authorization prompts.\\n\\nSTATE MODEL: Represent VM state as
      running/stopped/missing/starting/stopping/unknown, never map transport or inspect failure to
      stopped. Attach source and observed-at timestamps to every value and retain last-known values
      only with a STALE badge. Model quarantine independently as clear/quarantined(reason)/unknown.
      Model GitHub as metadata access, repository/configured_at, read-key presence, write-key
      presence, secret-binding state, and last verification separately; a present write token does
      not override read-only or missing metadata. After restore, mark GitHub authorization stale
      until verified. Batch operations need per-workspace succeeded/failed/not-run states. Timeouts,
      lost subprocesses, and failures after an external mutation begins must say outcome unknown and
      trigger read-only reconciliation, never automatic replay.\\n\\nCONCURRENCY: Only GitHub
      setup/verify/remove are serialized, and only per workspace; push, backup/restore, clean,
      resize, upgrade/update, host repair, tunnels, and exec have no repository-level coordination.
      Use a single-flight app action per workspace plus a global exclusive lock for
      backup/restore/update/host repair, disable conflicting controls, and refresh after every
      action. This cannot exclude external CLI activity, so preflight data must be considered
      advisory and destructive commands must revalidate at execution time.\\n\\nVerification was
      read-only: implementation, helper scripts, documentation, and focused test scenarios were
      cross-checked; git diff confirmed no changes to the audited files. Project-wide tests,
      formatters, and linters were not run as
      requested.\",\"filesChanged\":[]}","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}},"redaction"
      :{"applied":false,"ruleIds":[]},"annotations":{}} agent-session=0ms
